    "summary": "High‑energy April Fool’s show with stellar China>Rider and epic Estimated suite",
    "blurb": "The night kicks off with a prank Promised Land opening before launching into solid first‑set jams. The second set shines with a rolling China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, an expansive Estimated Prophet → He's Gone → The Other One suite, and a sweet Shakedown Street encore. Jerry’s lead work is on fire throughout.",
    "review": "April 1 1980 at the Capitol Theater captured one of the Dead’s most playful evenings. After the infamous April Fool’s gag – a “Promised Land” swap that saw Jerry, Brent and Mickey all taking turns behind the kit – the band settled into a tight first set highlighted by a punchy ‘Candyman’ and a breezy ‘Friend Of The Devil’.  The second set is where the show truly soars: a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider launch rolls straight into an inspired Estimated Prophet, which mutates into a blistering He’s Gone → The Other One marathon that many fans cite as a rare high‑point.  A tight ‘Feel Like A Stranger’, a soulful ‘Stella Blue’, and the crowd‑pleasing “Around & Around” close out the set before a sweet Shakedown Street encore caps the night.\n\nAcross the ten available sources, the Miller soundboard tapes (especially gd80-04-01.sbd.miller.12386.sbeok.shnf) are praised for crystal‑clear mix and no dropouts, making them ideal listening companions.  The community’s favorite, however, remains the Bertha‑Ashley SBD (gd80-04-01.sbd.bertha-ashley.26178.sbeok.shnf), which balances good fidelity with the historic April Fool’s moment that fans love.  Whether you’re after pristine audio or the raw joy of a prank‑filled set, this show delivers a memorable blend of humor, tight musicianship, and those classic Dead improvisational leaps.",
